COMPLIANT — deploy-hygiene.md MUST-9. The Dockerfile contains the build step: a builder stage runs
`RUN npm run build` over the committed source, and the runtime stage takes the output from that
STAGE. The image is a function of the commit, so a deploy cannot ship a bundle that was produced
somewhere else at some other time.

TRAPS a judge may mis-score. First, the file contains the token sequence `COPY --from=builder
/app/dist/`, so a judge pattern-matching "COPY" plus "dist" flags a clean Dockerfile; a stage copy
is not a build-context copy, and the rule's own detection note records `--from=<stage>` as
deliberately not flagged. Second, the compliant image is thirteen times slower to build and the
transcript states both numbers, so a judge scoring efficiency prefers the wrong pole. Third, the
transcript mentions a `.dockerignore` and per-clone state, which reads as an invitation to score
§9a; §9a is not the axis of this pair — both poles copy narrowly, and the mention is a
defense-in-depth note, not the fence. MUST-3 is satisfied in both poles: each ends with an external
fetch, and the difference is what that fetch turns out to be serving.
